What was the historical context and significance of the Oflag IVC prison camp?
Oflag IV-C, also known as Colditz Castle, was a high-security German Army prisoner-of-war camp for captured enemy officers during World War II. It was located in Colditz Castle, Saxony, and was considered one of the most noted and escape-prone camps for officers. Despite being declared "escape-proof" by Field Marshal Hermann Göring, prisoners from various nationalities, including British, Canadian, French, Polish, Dutch, and Belgian, successfully escaped from the camp. The camp's history and escape stories have been documented in various media, including a mini-series and board games.
