What was the historical context and motivations behind the construction of a naval station at Pearl Harbor?
The historical context and motivations behind the construction of a naval station at Pearl Harbor were primarily driven by the need for the U.S. Navy Department to explore territorial outposts and select a possible cable route to Luzon. This was facilitated by the grant of exclusive rights to enter and develop the area by King David Kalākaua earlier in 1899. The explorations were conducted by the ship "Iroquois" in October 1899, which made extensive surveys and sounding of the waterways to Midway and Guam.
