What is the geological process of metamorphosis, and how does it relate to the formation of marble?
Metamorphism is the geological process of changing the mineral composition and structure of rocks under high pressure and temperature conditions, without melting them. In the context of marble formation, metamorphism causes the recrystallization of the original carbonate mineral grains, resulting in the formation of an interlocking mosaic of carbonate crystals. This process modifies or destroys the primary sedimentary textures and structures of the original rock, transforming it into marble.
