What are the historical and linguistic origins of the words 'auld', 'lang', and 'syne' in the context of the Scottish poem 'Auld Lang Syne'?
In the context of the Scottish poem "Auld Lang Syne", the words "auld", "lang", and "syne" have historical and linguistic origins rooted in the Scots language, a Germanic language closely related to Old English and Northumbrian Old Norse.

- "Auld" is derived from the Scots word "auld", which is a variant of the Old English word "ald", meaning "old". This is cognate with the Modern English word "old".
- "Lang" is derived from the Scots word "lang", which is a variant of the Old English word "lang", meaning "long" or "length of time". This is cognate with the Modern English word "long".
- "Syne" is derived from the Scots word "syne", which is a variant of the Old English word "sīne", meaning "since" or "ago". This is cognate with the Modern English word "since".

In the context of the poem, "Auld Lang Syne" roughly translates to "old long since" or "days gone by", capturing the nostalgic and reflective tone of the poem, which is traditionally sung on New Year's Eve to bid farewell to the old year and welcome the new one.
