What is the history of public libraries in the UK, specifically focusing on the establishment of the first public library?
The history of public libraries in the UK began to take shape in the 19th century. The Public Libraries Act of 1850 allowed cities with populations exceeding 10,000 to levy taxes for the support of public libraries. The first unconditionally free public library in England was the Salford Museum and Art Gallery, which opened in November 1850 as "The Royal Museum & Public Library". However, the first library to operate a "free" lending library without subscription was the library in Campfield, Manchester, which started in 1852.

Prior to this, the Francis Trigge Chained Library in Grantham, Lincolnshire, established in 1598, is considered the forerunner of later public library systems, as it was one of the first libraries to be endowed for the benefit of users who were not members of an institution.

The modern concept of free, open-access libraries in the UK began to take shape in 1847, when Parliament appointed a committee led by William Ewart to consider the necessity of establishing libraries through the nation. Their report in 1849 noted the poor condition of library service and recommended the establishment of free public libraries all over the country.
