What is the cultural and mythological context of leprechauns in Irish folklore?
Leprechauns are a type of fairy of the Aos Sí in Irish folklore, typically depicted as little bearded men who partake in mischief. They are solitary creatures who spend their time making and mending shoes and have a hidden pot of gold at the end of the rainbow. According to Irish folklore, if captured by a human, they often grant three wishes in exchange for their freedom. Leprechauns are believed to be derived from the Tuatha Dé Danann and are a significant part of Irish mythology, with a large presence in Irish folklore.
